  "query_preview": "..Clear skies this afternoon from Tucson westward with \nscattered cumulus clouds this afternoon to the south and east of \nTucson. Temperatures remain a couple of degrees below normal. The \nupper level pattern currently features broad troughiness across the \narea as a weak low traverses east across northern Baja. Very little \nchange in our weather is expected through Saturday as the \naforementioned low shears out to our south with our area under weak \nwest to southwest flow as dry conditions and near normal \ntemperatures prevail. \n\nHeading into Sunday and especially Monday, another weather system \nwill impact the area as it moves from off the southern California \ncoast Sunday into SE CA and then NW Arizona on Monday. Southerly \nflow ahead of this will will help pull in some modest moisture (PWAT \n0.6\"-0.8\") from the south as early as Sunday evening but especially \novernight Sunday night and Monday. The combination of this increased \nmoisture and dynamics from the negative tilt trough will be just \nenough to result in a slight chance of showers Sunday night, then a \nchance of showers and thunderstorms on Monday (20 to 60 percent \nchance), though this will mainly be focused east of Tucson as the \ntiming of the trough sped up slightly. Given this timing and the \ndynamics from the trough, the best potential for storms is in Graham \nand Greenlee County. QPF values will remain low (mainly less than \n0.10\") with gusty winds with any showers and thunderstorms. Outside \nof any storms, with a tighter gradient, we'll see some breezes \nMonday area-wide with gusts up to around 25-30 mph. \n\nAny lingering moisture will get pushed out of our area by Tuesday \nmorning, with any slight chances of precipitation just ending across \nthe White Mountains Tuesday morning, with dry conditions elsewhere. \n\nSome breezes will continue through the middle of next week as the \narea remains under west to southwest flow aloft. We are seeing some \ndifferences in the ensembles by around next Wednesday/Thursday as \nanother trough from approaches from the northwest in terms of how \namplified it is, though that doesn't look to play a huge role for us \nas the tightest gradients look to remain to our northwest. \nTemperatures will trend towards slightly below normal levels the \nmiddle of next week.",
